      "externalId": "orig:math-int-002",
      "topicId": "math.integral.indefinite",
      "type": "single",
      "stem": "$\\displaystyle \\int x e^x\\,dx$ equals",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "$x e^x + C$",
          "whyWrong": "Differentiate it: you get $e^x + xe^x$, not $xe^x$. The second part of integration by parts is missing."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "$e^x(x - 1) + C$"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "$e^x(x + 1) + C$",
          "misconceptionId": "math.integral.indefinite.parts-sign",
          "whyWrong": "The $-\\int v\\,du$ term was added instead of subtracted."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "$\\tfrac{x^2}{2} e^x + C$",
          "whyWrong": "$e^x$ was treated as a constant. You can't integrate a product factor by factor."
        }
      ],
      "answer": {
        "kind": "choice",
        "keys": [
          "B"
        ]
      },
      "solution": "**Key idea:** integration by parts with $u = x$, $dv = e^x dx$.\n\n$\\int x e^x dx = x e^x - \\int e^x dx = x e^x - e^x + C =$ **$e^x(x - 1) + C$**\n\nCheck by differentiating: $e^x(x-1) + e^x = xe^x$ ✓",
      "hints": [
        "Choose $u = x$ (it becomes simpler when differentiated)."
      ],
